<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lydians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lydians were the people of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. They were known for their cavalry and wealth, and for being among the first to mint coins. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s conquest of Lydia, Lydians became subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, which drew on its subject peoples for military service.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lydians.jpg" alt="Lydians" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caliniuc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lydia_circa_50_AD_-_English_legend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Lydians were an Anatolian people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, a region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, who spoke the Lydian language, an Indo-European language of the Anatolian group. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> knew them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparda">Sparda</a>. Their origins, which reach well into the second millennium BCE, are still debated by language historians and archaeologists. Greek writers record semi-mythical dynasties, the Atyads or Tantalids and the Tylonids, before the last dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnads</a>, and a Lydian nation may have emerged from earlier identities as Maeonians and Luvians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Lydian capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, called Sfard in Lydian. Their recorded statehood spans three dynasties traceable to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> and reached its height in the seventh and sixth centuries BCE, as neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, to the north-east, declined. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who may have drawn on the Lydian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a> began when Gyges replaced King Kandaules, the last of the Tylonids, with the help of Kandaules's wife, who became his queen.</p><p>Lydian power ended abruptly with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> in 585 BCE and defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> in 546 BCE. A distinct Lydian culture probably lasted until shortly before the Common Era; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> records it last at Kibyra in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in the first century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports that a Lydian girl not born to the nobility practised prostitution until she had earned a dowry, and then announced her availability for marriage. He also credits the Lydians with inventing various games, notably knucklebones, which he says grew popular during a severe drought, when they gave relief from hardship.</p><p>Lydian texts known today are few and short, dating from about the mid-seventh century BCE to the second century BCE; one scholar concludes that the Lydians wrote early but not much. Some Lydian religious ideas may go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> or Late Stone Age, among them the vegetation goddess Kore, a snake and bull cult, a thunder and rain god with the double axe as his sign, and the mountain mother goddess assisted by lions. Contact with Greek religion lasted over a millennium. Artimu (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>) and Qλdãns (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>) have strong Anatolian elements, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a> and Paki (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysos</a>) passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>Lydian stories, among them the ring of Gyges, entered Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> collected many expressions about Lydians in his Adagia. The Lydian mode of ancient Greek music took its name from them, although the term now names a different mode.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lydians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its core was the Persian infantry and cavalry, supplemented by contingents from every subject nation, including Medes, Babylonians, Egyptians, <b>Lydians</b> and Indians, which made it the most ethnically diverse army the ancient world had seen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus also claimed that the <b>Lydians</b> were the first retail traders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus relates that under Sadyattes and then Alyattes the <b>Lydians</b> invaded Milesian territory every year for eleven years, burning the crops, but could not take the city because Miletus controlled the sea; the tyrant Thrasybulus finally persuaded Alyattes to make peace by staging a show of plenty when…</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around c. 635 BC the Scythians under Madyes and the <b>Lydians</b> under Alyattes defeated them for good.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
